'''''Alchemilla''''' is a genus of herbaceous perennial plants in the family [[Rosaceae]], with the common name '''lady's mantle''' applied generically as well as specifically to ''[[Alchemilla mollis]]'' when referred to as a garden plant. The plant used as a herbal tea or for medicinal usage such as gynaecological disorders is ''[[Alchemilla xanthochlora]]'' or in Middle Europe the so-called common lady's mantle ''[[Alchemilla vulgaris]]''. There are between 800 and 1000 species, the majority native to cool temperate and subarctic regions of Europe and Asia, with a few species native to the mountains of Africa and the Americas.<ref>{{cite web |title=''Alchemilla'' L. |work=Plants of the World Online |publisher=Royal Botanic Gardens, Kew |url=https://powo.science.kew.org/taxon/urn:lsid:ipni.org:names:30008181-2 |access-date=2026-08-23}}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Jakimiuk |first1=Katarzyna |last2=Tomczyk |first2=Michał |title=A review of the traditional uses, phytochemistry, pharmacology, and clinical evidence for the use of the genus ''Alchemilla'' (Rosaceae) |journal=Journal of Ethnopharmacology |volume=320 |year=2024 |article-number=117439 |doi=10.1016/j.jep.2023.117439 |url=https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0378874123013090|doi-access=free }}</ref>

Most species of ''Alchemilla'' are clump-forming or mounded perennials with basal leaves arising from woody rhizomes. Some species have leaves with lobes that radiate from a common point and others have divided leaves—both are typically fan-shaped with small teeth at the tips. The long-stalked, gray-green to green leaves are often covered with soft hairs, and show a high degree of water-resistance (see [[Lotus effect]]). Green to bright chartreuse flowers are small, have no petals and appear in clusters above the foliage in late spring and summer.

